{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the hare</b> — It is a species that is so called in the sacred language for both the male and the female, and likewise the ostrich. And among birds, the dove — the name of the male is not distinct from the female. And there are many whose names are in the masculine and their females have no other name: גמל (camel), שפן (hyrax), חזיר (pig), \"a watchful bear\" (Lamentations 3:10), \"cow and bear shall graze\" (Isaiah 11:7). And likewise the תור (turtledove) among birds, and therefore Scripture says (Leviticus 5:7) \"two תורים or two sons of יונה\" — for the תורים it uses the masculine form שתים, and for the יונה it says שני, to make known that there is no distinction between them. And do not object to me from the language of our Sages, who say (Bechorot 28a) פרה וחזירה (cow and sow), and who say (ibid. 6a) גמל הבא מן הגמלה (a camel coming from a female camel), for they impose upon them a borrowed expression to clarify their intent. And the reason for the prohibition in these is that they do not possess both signs."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:40.920461565Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}